Z||000000||olliver||10-29-2001||02:44 PM||||I'm just wondering what suspension parts/companies people are using to lower their fiero.  I'm going to lower mine this winter and i'm surveying to find out who has the best product.  So if you've slammed your baby please tell me what you've used and your personal opinions whether they be good or bad. <P>Thanx,<BR>Olliver<P>P.S. has anyone ever heard of this company?http://www.ground-control.com/index.htm<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||TRiAD||10-29-2001||04:43 PM||||Ground Control are a very competent company. They utilize Eibach springs, and just may have adjustable shocks/struts for you as well.<P>Shoot me an email once you get a quote from them and I should be able to beat it for you...<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||10-29-2001||08:37 PM||||Ground control ...Had em ..Modified them to work right.<P>Too short a setup. <P>wanna do it with a longer travel spring ? more adjustability and cheaper?<BR> <A HREF="http://www.stockcarproducts.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.stockcarproducts.com/</A> <P>Get the 7" tall carrera sleeve. tell them you want an ID of 2.5" (Koni's measure 2.5 OD" measure your strut for assurance)<P>The sleeves are $36 and a lot longer than the Ground control sleeve. You will have to cut the collar off the strut and relocate it further down in accordance with the sleeve height. The ground control setup has the sleeve on the perch , and the sleeve has to be modified so that it doesn't teater on the perch..and it will under loads.<P>Then choose a spring ..from 275lbs to 325lbs depedning on your driving taste. 325lbs is very very heavy for the street. Anyone who has ridden in my car can attest to this.<P>Hope this helps. It's a bit more work , but the result is a better setup with more adjustability.<P>Happy Slamming. (need to change my sig , should say carrera coilovers)<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||10-30-2001||09:05 AM||||If your looking at slamming an '84 to '87, I'd recommend <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream" TARGET=_blank>Street Dreams</A> 2" front drop spindles.  They maintain the stock suspension geometry and travels.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue4.gif">     <BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR><A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/tatooine/spielberg/241/" TARGET=_blank>Tigger's Fiero Page</A><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
